1278 0 obj
  << /ActualText (What I do in my own projects is explore the subculture of outcomes of these technologies\056 Gibson has a great quote that he uses when he talks about the way that he thinks about technology in his books\054 which is \034the street find its own uses for things\035\056 So the point that he\047s most interested in with new technology is when it hits the street and it becomes democratised\056 That\047s when it gets used and reapplied in strange and unexpected ways\056 And that\047s really where we site a lot of our own film work through the narrative development \055 in thinking about the ways in which sub\055cultures might start to work with and use these technologies\056\nSo in \030Where the City Can\047t See\031\054 we looked at rave culture\056 We developed a new vocabulary of dance movements that are designed to evade body detection algorithms\056 We developed new textiles and a new hoody that\047s designed to create glitches and distortions when it\047s seen through the eyes of a driverless car or through CCTV laser scanners\056 We worked with Adam Harvey an artist that designs make up systems to fool facial recognition technologies\056 And those same textiles were used \030In the Robot Skies\031 to create hoodies that would evade drone based camera systems\056\nThey\031re not just storytelling elements they\047re inventions\056 We\047re designing products\056 They become very real when you engage the technology and inhabit the fictions themselves in this way\056)
     /K [ 60 ]
     /P 1279 0 R
     /Pg 1158 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

